1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,039
Welcome to PlotTwist, a fictional mystery experience business.

2
00:00:05,039 --> 00:00:08,678
This is the C3 business assistant walkthrough.

3
00:00:08,678 --> 00:00:16,011
It shows the working interface, message routing, memory, local tools, and reflection.

4
00:00:16,011 --> 00:00:19,990
The captured conversations use offline demo mode.

5
00:00:19,990 --> 00:00:28,033
Real OpenAI integration is implemented in the code, but no live API call is shown in this video.

6
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:35,496
The business offers Birthday Mystery Nights, City Quests, Corporate Missions, and Custom Story Adventures.

7
00:00:35,496 --> 00:00:40,845
Package details come from the text summary and four-page PDF dossier.

8
00:00:40,845 --> 00:00:44,678
Prices are illustrative starting prices per group.

9
00:00:44,678 --> 00:00:53,351
Behind the Plot introduces Aseel, Noah, Jiso, and Felix, and provides the business PDF for download.

10
00:00:53,351 --> 00:00:59,600
The persona and operating instructions are clearly separated in prompts dot py and in the notebook.

11
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:02,529
Clue sounds warm and curious.

12
00:01:02,529 --> 00:01:13,036
The instructions require grounded answers, missing-field collection, honest handling of unknowns, and acknowledgement only after successful tool results.

13
00:01:13,036 --> 00:01:19,215
These two labeled blocks are assembled with the business knowledge into the system prompt.

14
00:01:19,215 --> 00:01:28,167
Before answering, the agent classifies the incoming message into general question, lead collection, or feedback and unanswered question.

15
00:01:28,167 --> 00:01:33,936
This implementation uses lightweight heuristics and recent conversation context.

16
00:01:33,936 --> 00:01:37,475
The latest activity panel exposes the route.

17
00:01:37,475 --> 00:01:47,767
Because the router is heuristic, unfamiliar wording can fall into the unanswered path; live model quality still needs separate evaluation.

18
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:51,361
I ask, What adventures do you offer?

19
00:01:51,361 --> 00:01:56,735
Clue describes the four experiences and their known limits and starting prices.

20
00:01:56,735 --> 00:01:59,874
The activity shows general question.

21
00:01:59,874 --> 00:02:05,307
The knowledge trace identifies the text summary and PDF as loaded sources.

22
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:12,045
The answer invites a useful next step without inventing availability or confirming a booking.

23
00:02:12,045 --> 00:02:17,274
Next, I request contact about a birthday mystery for ten people.

24
00:02:17,274 --> 00:02:20,203
The route changes to lead collection.

25
00:02:20,203 --> 00:02:26,266
Clue asks for the missing name and email address while keeping the event brief from the previous turn.

26
00:02:26,266 --> 00:02:34,714
This demonstrates short-term conversational memory: the customer does not need to repeat all of the event details.

27
00:02:34,714 --> 00:02:42,067
I supply the fictional name Sam Rivera, the example email address, and permission to save the enquiry.

28
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:46,636
The agent combines these details with the earlier event request.

29
00:02:46,636 --> 00:02:50,780
The customer interest tool writes an actual local record.

30
00:02:50,780 --> 00:02:57,028
Only after its successful result does Clue confirm that the enquiry was saved.

31
00:02:57,028 --> 00:03:03,556
The required function is record customer interest, with email, name, and message parameters.

32
00:03:03,556 --> 00:03:10,534
Its documented schema rejects extra fields, and the implementation validates the supplied details.

33
00:03:10,534 --> 00:03:18,558
Here is the captured tool result from this run: success is true, the kind is lead, and the status is saved.

34
00:03:18,558 --> 00:03:22,852
Local logging also prints the tool event.

35
00:03:22,852 --> 00:03:27,465
I report that the instructions at the start of the mystery felt rushed.

36
00:03:27,465 --> 00:03:31,314
The router selects feedback and unanswered question.

37
00:03:31,314 --> 00:03:38,237
The second required function, record feedback, receives the question text and saves it locally.

38
00:03:38,237 --> 00:03:46,750
The visible arguments and returned record show that this is a real persistence action, even though the demo reply is scripted.

39
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:50,009
I ask about the cancellation policy.

40
00:03:50,009 --> 00:03:53,798
That policy is absent from the business profile.

41
00:03:53,798 --> 00:03:59,741
Clue admits the information gap and records the unanswered question through the feedback tool.

42
00:03:59,741 --> 00:04:05,015
It does not manufacture terms or promise that somebody received a message.

43
00:04:05,015 --> 00:04:09,769
The record is available for local review in the casebook.

44
00:04:09,769 --> 00:04:13,327
Reflection is enabled before the final reply.

45
00:04:13,327 --> 00:04:20,651
This captured audit shows the original draft, revised reply, checks, and a changed flag.

46
00:04:20,651 --> 00:04:24,955
The draft already passed, so changed is false.

47
00:04:24,955 --> 00:04:28,924
Offline mode uses a deterministic guardrail audit.

48
00:04:28,924 --> 00:04:37,537
Live mode uses a second model request to review and revise the draft when necessary; the code logs both versions.

49
00:04:37,537 --> 00:04:43,655
The casebook contains the lead, feedback, and unanswered question from this browser session.

50
00:04:43,655 --> 00:04:47,244
Refresh retrieves only this session’s records.

51
00:04:47,244 --> 00:04:52,318
Export creates a JSON file that can be downloaded and checked.

52
00:04:52,318 --> 00:04:55,722
SQLite provides local persistence.

53
00:04:55,722 --> 00:05:03,625
Starting a new browser session changes the visible casebook while earlier records remain in the local database.

54
00:05:03,625 --> 00:05:13,613
The live adapter passes the system prompt, current route, chat history, user message, and tool schemas to OpenAI Chat Completions.

55
00:05:13,613 --> 00:05:18,231
A model tool request is executed by the validated registry.

56
00:05:18,231 --> 00:05:23,784
Its result is appended with the matching tool call identifier and returned to the model.

57
00:05:23,784 --> 00:05:30,023
If no tool is requested, the generated text becomes the draft reply.

58
00:05:30,023 --> 00:05:37,106
The main notebook contains readable persona, instructions, schemas, and agent implementation cells.

59
00:05:37,106 --> 00:05:41,975
It reconstructs the supporting files in a separate notebook workspace.

60
00:05:41,975 --> 00:05:46,199
The launch section creates the designed Gradio interface.

61
00:05:46,199 --> 00:05:50,297
Set Run UI to true after running the earlier cells.

62
00:05:50,297 --> 00:05:55,506
The folder version also launches directly from app dot py.

63
00:05:55,506 --> 00:05:59,880
This public Hugging Face Space is running the free Static edition.

64
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:03,684
I enter a business question and receive a response.

65
00:06:03,684 --> 00:06:10,012
It includes browser-based routing, local records, and a rule-based reflection check.

66
00:06:10,012 --> 00:06:20,415
This free edition uses JavaScript and scripted replies; it does not host the full Python agent, Gradio backend, or live OpenAI calls.

67
00:06:20,415 --> 00:06:29,003
To try C3 locally, create a virtual environment, install the requirements, and launch app dot py in demo mode.

68
00:06:29,003 --> 00:06:33,782
The default address uses port 7860.

69
00:06:33,782 --> 00:06:40,730
For live mode, privately configure your API key in the local environment file and change the mode.

70
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:47,729
Fifteen behavioral tests pass, including controlled model tool-round-trip and reflection checks.

71
00:06:47,729 --> 00:06:52,347
These tests do not measure live model quality.

72
00:06:52,347 --> 00:06:57,406
C3 is submitted as a GitHub repository and a functionality video.

73
00:06:57,406 --> 00:07:03,164
Include the notebook, business sources, requirements, README, and working code.

74
00:07:03,164 --> 00:07:09,278
This walkthrough documents the offline functionality and explains the implemented live path.

75
00:07:09,278 --> 00:07:15,506
Before presenting real model behavior as tested, configure the key and record a live run.

76
00:07:15,506 --> 00:07:21,944
The Static Space supplements the project; it does not replace the full agent.

77
00:07:21,944 --> 00:07:25,158
Now, the C4 Persona Lab.

78
00:07:25,158 --> 00:07:32,611
It extends the same fictional business with three distinct agent personas and a manually assembled LangGraph action loop.

79
00:07:32,611 --> 00:07:42,879
This section shows configuration controls, search and estimate tools, persona comparison, records, experiments, and reflection.

80
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:51,482
The captured responses are offline simulations running through the real graph, not measurements of a live language model.

81
00:07:51,482 --> 00:07:55,571
Clue helps first-time guests select an experience.

82
00:07:55,571 --> 00:07:59,795
Cipher checks group capacity, budget, and duration.

83
00:07:59,795 --> 00:08:04,988
Muse develops original story concepts within known business constraints.

84
00:08:04,988 --> 00:08:10,207
Each persona has its own prompt, task, settings, and rationale.

85
00:08:10,207 --> 00:08:23,479
The configuration file starts Clue at temperature 0.45, Cipher at 0.10, and Muse at 0.85, with different penalties and output budgets.

86
00:08:23,479 --> 00:08:34,162
The interface lets me choose a persona, direct or brief-plan responses, zero-shot or few-shot examples, and system or in-message instruction placement.

87
00:08:34,162 --> 00:08:41,476
Sampling controls include temperature, top-p, penalties, and maximum output tokens.

88
00:08:41,476 --> 00:08:44,800
Switching persona loads its defaults.

89
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:54,587
Offline mode demonstrates the control wiring; changing sampling values does not turn the scripted simulator into a stochastic model.

90
00:08:54,587 --> 00:09:00,406
I ask Cipher to plan a birthday mystery for ten people with a three-hundred-dollar budget.

91
00:09:00,406 --> 00:09:07,799
The graph searches the catalogue, estimates the selected package, and produces a grounded planning brief.

92
00:09:07,799 --> 00:09:16,512
Birthday Mystery Nights fits ten guests, lasts one hundred twenty minutes, and starts at two hundred forty dollars per group.

93
00:09:16,512 --> 00:09:21,656
Final scope and availability still require human review.

94
00:09:21,656 --> 00:09:27,824
The trace exposes an action with tool arguments, then the actual returned observation.

95
00:09:27,824 --> 00:09:31,773
A second action estimates the selected experience.

96
00:09:31,773 --> 00:09:39,536
The observation reports capacity, duration, and starting price, and the next step produces the final reply.

97
00:09:39,536 --> 00:09:43,830
The brief plan is a concise explanation of the next action.

98
00:09:43,830 --> 00:09:50,008
It is not private model reasoning or evidence of a live model thinking process.

99
00:09:50,008 --> 00:09:56,676
I start a fresh chat with Muse and request the same birthday plan, adding an original story hook.

100
00:09:56,676 --> 00:10:03,139
The answer proposes a missing-alibi concept where each guest contributes one page to the final reveal.

101
00:10:03,139 --> 00:10:07,843
The package size, duration, and starting price remain the same.

102
00:10:07,843 --> 00:10:14,257
The imagined story is labeled as a concept rather than a confirmed service inclusion.

103
00:10:14,257 --> 00:10:20,815
Persona Studio sends the shared brief to fresh conversations for Clue, Cipher, and Muse.

104
00:10:20,815 --> 00:10:23,734
Clue asks about the atmosphere.

105
00:10:23,734 --> 00:10:26,838
Cipher lists practical checks.

106
00:10:26,838 --> 00:10:31,402
Muse offers a story concept and personalization question.

107
00:10:31,402 --> 00:10:36,161
The side-by-side display keeps the factual baseline comparable.

108
00:10:36,161 --> 00:10:45,898
Here, these style differences are scripted demonstrations; the display does not establish which real model persona performs best.

109
00:10:45,898 --> 00:10:52,712
I provide the fictional customer name, email address, and permission to save the event enquiry.

110
00:10:52,712 --> 00:11:01,385
The graph selects record customer interest, validates the arguments, writes a local record, and returns the observation.

111
00:11:01,385 --> 00:11:04,599
The final reply acknowledges storage.

112
00:11:04,599 --> 00:11:10,942
It explicitly avoids claiming that an email was sent or a reservation was created.

113
00:11:10,942 --> 00:11:15,166
Feedback about difficult puzzles triggers the feedback tool.

114
00:11:15,166 --> 00:11:23,124
A later question about wheelchair access and next Friday is also logged because the catalogue cannot verify those details.

115
00:11:23,124 --> 00:11:29,832
The graph returns a successful storage observation, and the assistant states the information gap.

116
00:11:29,832 --> 00:11:37,035
These cases show the boundary between a known business fact and a detail that needs team confirmation.

117
00:11:37,035 --> 00:11:40,304
I lower the budget to one hundred dollars.

118
00:11:40,304 --> 00:11:47,332
The estimate still reports the real catalogue starting price of two hundred forty dollars for the birthday package.

119
00:11:47,332 --> 00:11:51,616
Cipher explicitly says that this exceeds the stated budget.

120
00:11:51,616 --> 00:11:58,719
The answer preserves the constraint rather than changing the price to make the recommendation seem feasible.

121
00:11:58,719 --> 00:12:06,802
The C4 casebook shows the saved customer enquiry, puzzle feedback, and unanswered accessibility question from this session.

122
00:12:06,802 --> 00:12:10,851
Export creates a downloadable JSON file.

123
00:12:10,851 --> 00:12:15,344
The local records preserve the actual payloads and timestamps.

124
00:12:15,344 --> 00:12:25,022
The same storage boundary applies: the records support a classroom review, and no external customer service workflow is triggered.

125
00:12:25,022 --> 00:12:34,180
I switch to direct output, zero solved task examples, and in-message instruction placement to demonstrate the alternate controls.

126
00:12:34,180 --> 00:12:38,799
The experiment runner isolates these factors in separate variants.

127
00:12:38,799 --> 00:12:43,412
A fixed protocol example stays in both example conditions.

128
00:12:43,412 --> 00:12:52,635
The role comparison moves the experimental instruction block while essential business and protocol boundaries remain in the system message.

129
00:12:52,635 --> 00:13:01,058
The implementation creates model, parse, action, recover, and finish nodes in a LangGraph StateGraph.

130
00:13:01,058 --> 00:13:05,542
The parser selects an action, recovery, or final path.

131
00:13:05,542 --> 00:13:09,416
Action observations feed back into the model node.

132
00:13:09,416 --> 00:13:13,170
A configured call bound prevents an endless loop.

133
00:13:13,170 --> 00:13:19,983
This graph uses the project’s own parser and registry rather than a prebuilt agent executor.

134
00:13:19,983 --> 00:13:27,396
The C4 system separates the chosen persona from detailed operating rules and the JSON action protocol.

135
00:13:27,396 --> 00:13:35,944
Its instructions cover grounded facts, missing information, customer permission, tool failures, and final response behavior.

136
00:13:35,944 --> 00:13:39,687
A minimal worked protocol example is included.

137
00:13:39,687 --> 00:13:43,926
Few-shot mode adds solved tool-use demonstrations.

138
00:13:43,926 --> 00:13:50,584
Invalid model outputs are handled by the recovery path and remain visible in the trace.

139
00:13:50,584 --> 00:13:59,982
The saved experiment matrix covers sixteen variants, seven cases, and two repeats, for two hundred twenty-four deterministic runs.

140
00:13:59,982 --> 00:14:07,310
Expected tool observations and completed responses are recorded for all runs, with zero tool errors.

141
00:14:07,310 --> 00:14:12,609
Raw traces, metrics, and comparison reports are included.

142
00:14:12,609 --> 00:14:20,982
These results check mechanics; they do not measure live-model creativity, naturalness, or prompt quality.

143
00:14:20,982 --> 00:14:31,745
The reflection document addresses persona helpfulness, prompt and configuration performance, tool reasoning, instruction effects, and implementation challenges.

144
00:14:31,745 --> 00:14:35,708
The notebook includes the reflection content as well.

145
00:14:35,708 --> 00:14:41,617
It separates design rationale from measured results and does not invent a live-model winner.

146
00:14:41,617 --> 00:14:49,660
After real API experiments, add measured findings and human ratings to support the final comparison.

147
00:14:49,660 --> 00:14:57,018
C4 launches with the same environment setup, normally on port 7861.

148
00:14:57,018 --> 00:15:06,631
The behavioral suite passes twenty-three tests, including parsing, tools, failures, persistence, and loop bounds.

149
00:15:06,631 --> 00:15:13,299
To evaluate real model behavior, configure the private key and run the live experiment command.

150
00:15:13,299 --> 00:15:22,072
Review raw outputs, complete human ratings, and preserve the run mode and model configuration with every result.

151
00:15:22,072 --> 00:15:32,814
The repository contains two separate project folders, executable notebooks, business sources, READMEs, requirements, tests, and experiment artifacts.

152
00:15:32,814 --> 00:15:36,898
C4 also includes its five-question reflection.

153
00:15:36,898 --> 00:15:43,751
Use the chapter videos to review functionality, and run the notebooks to inspect the implementation.

154
00:15:43,751 --> 00:15:53,689
This edited walkthrough documents actual offline actions; a live API run and measured C4 comparisons remain the final evidence to add.
